panvk: a colour input attachment of the subpass's own is read from the tile buffer, in rasterization order (JM: Bifrost)

Applied by tools/mk-panvk.sh only, after tools/mesa/*.patch and the panvk patches before it, to Mesa 25.0.7 (Mesa's own licence,
MIT, applies to the lines it adds). Written for Scion.

Why. metal2vulkan reads Metal's framebuffer fetch ([[color(n)]]; CoreAnimation's window server reads its colour attachment so in its
luma boost and blend shaders) as an input attachment of the colour attachment itself. PanVK read every input attachment through memory
(nir_lower_input_attachments: a texel fetch at the fragment's coordinate), so it could not offer rasterization-order access, and
metal2vulkan's unordered form (tools/metal/m2v/0005) records a barrier inside the render pass before each fetching draw that follows a
colour write. On JM a barrier ends the batch: every tile is written out, and the next batch loads it again
(cmd_preload_fb_after_batch_split). The window server's frames were split so 5 to 13 times each: 3240 of 5052 fragment batches of a run of
home-screen swipes came from such barriers (counted by a test build), and a frame of several passes took a median 52 ms from commit to
completion, most of it the submission of the many job chains.

What. Bifrost reads the tile buffer in the shader (LD_TILE, through load_output: bifrost_preprocess_nir makes it a
load_converted_output_pan, as for the blend shaders and OpenGL ES's framebuffer fetch), and orders a fragment's tile buffer accesses
after those of the earlier fragments at its pixel, as its blending is.
  - panvk_physical_device.c: VK_EXT_rasterization_order_attachment_access and rasterizationOrderColorAttachmentAccess on v6 and v7
    (JM); depth and stencil access stay unordered (not offered).
  - panvk_vX_shader.c (PAN_ARCH <= 7): input attachments are lowered when a shader is compiled, where the pipeline's state says which
    colour attachment each input attachment index is (vk_input_attachment_location_state, which the runtime fills for a VkRenderPass
    subpass too) and its format. An image load of a subpass input at offset (0, 0) whose index is colour attachment N's becomes a
    load_output of FRAG_RESULT_DATA0 + N; the render target's conversion is inlined from its format (pan_inline_rt_conversion, as for
    the blend shaders). A multisampled subpass input is lowered so where it is read at the invocation's own sample (load_sample_id:
    metal2vulkan reads a multisampled pass's attachments so, under sample shading); the shader then runs per sample. Every other input
    attachment (depth, stencil, another subpass's colours, another sample, an offset) is lowered by nir_lower_input_attachments as
    before, now in the same place. The shader then reads the tile buffer (outputs_read), which turns forward pixel kill off for its
    draws (pan_shader.c). The graphics state hash takes the input attachment map and the colour formats.
  - bifrost_compile.c: a fragment shader that runs per sample (uses_sample_shading) reads the tile buffer at its own sample
    (bi_pixel_indices, as a multisampled blend shader does); one that runs per pixel stays at sample 0.
Valhall and later (PAN_ARCH >= 9) are unchanged.

The first form of this patch (831377b, reverted in d03db94) lowered single-sampled subpass inputs only, yet offered rasterization order
for every colour attachment: VectorKit draws its map four times multisampled and reads its colour attachment in many shaders
(framebufferFetchSupport, fetch_color), and those reads went on through memory with no barrier, so the map's water came out speckled
and its rail lines black.

Checked on the phone (Mali-G72, PanVK by HAKIOS_VK_ICD): MapKit's map of a fixed public place (full screen, the drawable handed to the
display) is drawn as with PanVK without this patch, the water even and the rail lines grey (half-size shots of the two differ only
where labels were placed or fading in); metal_tri plain, --fetch (256, 1024, over an IOSurface),
--surface, --ycbcr, --xr over/add, --texel-buffer 16, 6912 --a8, 256 --blit, 64 --render and --surface --size 1125 --row-bytes 4608
--frames 30 pass. The window server: about 10 fragment job chains a frame while swiping (37 without), the home screen, its widgets and
the dock's material drawn as before; 22 to 23 frames a second through continuous home-page swipes against 18 (docs/metal.md).

